Percentage":0.7281,"playId":"401858291248","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7297,"playId":"401858291252","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.6997,"playId":"401858291256","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7127,"playId":"401858291260","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.724,"playId":"401858291264","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7242,"playId":"401858291270","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.782,"playId":"401858291268","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7792,"playId":"401858291273","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7847,"playId":"401858291276","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7835,"playId":"401858291280","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7846,"playId":"401858291284","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7977,"playId":"401858291289","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7956,"playId":"401858291292","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7866,"playId":"401858291296","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7732,"playId":"401858291299","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7957,"playId":"401858291304","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7962,"playId":"401858291310","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7638,"playId":"401858291308","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7529,"playId":"401858291313","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7645,"playId":"401858291317","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7923,"playId":"401858291321","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7806,"playId":"401858291324","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.781,"playId":"401858291328","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7785,"playId":"401858291335","tiePercentage":0}],"gameInfo":{"venue":{"id":"3855","guid":"ca633928-f7c4-3aa7-8fc1-69f984decc06","fullName":"Notre Dame Stadium","address":{"city":"Notre Dame","state":"IN","zipCode":"46556","country":"USA"},"grass":false,"images":[{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/college-football/day/3855.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day"]},{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/college-football/day/interior/3855.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day","interior"]}]},"attendance":77622},"article":{"headline":"No. 4 Notre Dame takes big lead, then holds off No. 1 Miami 31-24 in a classic","description":"— Marcus Freeman didn't deny the obvious. Even for a storied program like Notre Dame, beating Miami had enormous significance.","story":"SOUTH BEND, Ind. -- — <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5444869/marcus-freeman\" target=\"_new\">Marcus Freeman</a> didn't deny the obvious. Even for a storied program like Notre Dame, beating Miami had enormous significance.\n\n\r\n              If that wasn't enough, it kept Notre Dame's national title hopes alive as well.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5079369/cj-carr\" target=\"_new\">CJ Carr</a> threw for 319 yards and three touchdowns, and No. 4 Notre Dame avenged its most damaging loss from last season by running out to a 21-point lead and holding on to beat No. 1 Miami 31-24 on Saturday night.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5079475/micah-gilbert\" target=\"_new\">Micah Gilbert</a> caught two of Carr's scoring passes, <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5150424/jordan-faison\" target=\"_new\">Jordan Faison</a> had the other and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5189515/nolan-james-jr\" target=\"_new\">Nolan James Jr</a>. had a rushing score for Notre Dame (8-1, No. 11 College Football Playoff), which came into the game knowing another loss to Miami could doom its CFP chances.\n\n\r\n              “It’s a special moment,” said Freeman, Notre Dame's coach. “Enjoy the moment, because tomorrow when you wake up it’s time to get ready for the next one. I know it’s a big one. This is big. This is special. I’m not going to sit here and say this was like the other ones. No, this is different.”\n\n\r\n              The Hurricanes (8-1, No. 1 CFP) started slowly on offense in their first game without injured wide receiver and <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/malachi-toney-miami-ankle-82f152a01cbb49c85df566be45597bfb\">Heisman Trophy hopeful <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5159175/malachi-toney\" target=\"_new\">Malachi Toney</a></a> — and Notre Dame, <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/notre-dame-miami-football-score-f63f2d411e1a1f4c1ada7c0910d1de87\">which lost to Miami 27-24 last season</a> in a game that ultimately kept the Irish out of the playoff, never trailed.\n\n\r\n              “Our backs were against the wall,” said Irish defensive back <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5121819/luke-talich\" target=\"_new\">Luke Talich</a>, who had an interception and a critical — and disputed — pass breakup to help seal the win.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5121169/darian-mensah\" target=\"_new\">Darian Mensah</a> threw three touchdown passes for Miami, twice getting the Hurricanes within a touchdown after they trailed 24-3 early in the third. <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/4804878/cooper-barkate\" target=\"_new\">Cooper Barkate</a> had a pair of those scoring grabs, and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5079503/elija-lofton\" target=\"_new\">Elija Lofton</a> had the other.\n\n\r\n              “Proud of the guys, the way we fought back,” Mensah said. “Obviously, it wasn't enough.”\n\n\r\n              Lofton nearly had a touchdown catch that would have made it 31-30 — the score of the Miami-Notre Dame game in 1988 — with 3:36 left, appearing to make a fourth-down grab in the end zone and getting his feet down.\n\n\r\n              But Notre Dame's Luke Talich — who had an interception in the third quarter — knocked the ball away, with officials ruling that Lofton didn't complete the grab. Notre Dame took over on downs, ran the clock down to about a minute and held on from there.\n\n\r\n              Miami coach Mario Cristobal <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/miami-elija-lofton-catch-notre-dame-9fbe25a7a93bbb17631cacd24551e5b9\">said he was “beyond upset”</a> that Lofton's play wasn't ruled a score.\n\n\r\n              “Obviously, a tough loss to take,” Cristobal said. “Proud of our players for the resilience. ... Don't leave it in the hands of the officials. Play better. Coach better. Find a way to win.”\n\n\r\n              With Miami falling, only two unbeaten teams remain in the Football Bowl Subdivision — Utah and UCLA, which both moved to 9-0 on Saturday. This is the first time since 2003 that only two teams made it through the first nine games unbeaten; TCU and Oklahoma did it that year.\n\n\r\n              On a night when 43 NFL scouts were part of a jam-packed crowd at Notre Dame Stadium, where it was 40 degrees Fahrenheit at kickoff and fell into the mid-30s as the night went along, the Irish stayed true to their mantra for 2026 — one born from the frustration from not making the CFP field a year ago.\n\n\r\n              They left no doubt. It got a little shaky at the end, but the Irish's CFP hopes are very much alive.\n\n\r\n              “As good a win as I’ve ever been a part of,” Freeman said. “As special of a moment, as special of an outcome that I’ve been a part of since I’ve been the head coach here.”\n\n\r\n              <hl2>The takeaway</hl2>\r\n              Miami: Miami had first-and-goal from the Notre Dame 1 in the second quarter and wound up settling for a field goal, after three runs — one by Fletcher and two keepers by Mensah — went nowhere. The Hurricanes wound up trailing 17-3 at halftime, after scoring at least 14 points and averaging 27 first-half points in their first eight contests this season.\n\n\r\n              Notre Dame: The Irish pulled out all the stops, with a slew of former stars — Joe Montana, Jerome Bettis Sr., Tony Rice, Chris Zorich, Ricky Watters, Ron Powlus and NBA President of Basketball Operations Byron Spruell among them — for on-field tributes. Also honored was Pat Murphy, the manager of the World Series champion Milwaukee Brewers, who won 73% of his games when he coached at Notre Dame.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>No. 1 things</hl2>\r\n              Miami fell to 44-7 when playing as the No. 1 team in the AP Top 25, including an 0-2 mark against Notre Dame — after the top-ranked Hurricanes lost to the fourth-ranked Irish 31-30 in the “Catholics vs. Convicts” game in 1988.\n\n\r\n              Notre Dame improved to 10-17-1 against No. 1 teams, getting its first win in exactly six years. It beat No. 1 Clemson at home on Nov. 7, 2020, a game where the Tigers were without star quarterback Trevor Lawrence.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Up next</hl2>\r\n              Miami: Hosts Duke next Saturday.\n\n\r\n              Notre Dame: Hosts Boston College next Saturday.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"https://apnews.com/newsletters\">Sign up for Top 25 poll alerts here</a>. Visit <a href=\"https://apnews.com/hub/ap-top-25-college-football-poll\">the Top 25 here</a> and see AP’s full <a href=\"https://apnews.com/hub/college-football\">college football coverage here</a>","published":"2026-11-08T04:13:34Z"}}